  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 1100 PM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nBy Tuesday night, NW flow aloft develops over the eastern CONUS. A \nlongwave trough axis settles ESE via upper flow into Missouri and \nKentucky with a weaker trough axis hinted at that extends from the \neastern Ohio Valley SW into NW GA and NE AL. This forcing could \nproduce a low to medium chance of showers of thunderstorms just after\ndaybreak on Wednesday that increases in coverage as the main \nlongwave trough axis digs southeast during the afternoon and \nevening hours. This will likely keep highs a tad lower in the mid\n80s to around 90 degrees. Shear remains weak as this occurs, so \norganized severe weather is not expected. SBCAPE could be between \n2000 and 4000 J/KG though in the afternoon for storms to tap into.\nWith high PWATS continuing, heavy rainfall will continue to be a \nthreat with stronger storms that may develop. DCAPE values are \naround or over 1000 J/KG and soundings do hint at dry air aloft \nfor storms to tap into, so some stronger downbursts look possible\nwith this convection. Models differ on how long this convection \nlasts into the evening hours before dissipating, but would expect \nby 10 PM this would occur (some guidance holds on to activity \nuntil just after midnight). Not sure how much clearing we will see\nbased on model soundings into the daybreak hours on Thursday. \nThis lack of clearing despite light winds should keep fog from \ndeveloping.\n\nModels differ concerning how quickly the upper ridge builds \nnorthward Thursday afternoon and evening. Some keep a weak longwave \ntrough axis in place over southern Alabama NW into Missouri. This\nsolution would keep the area in a wetter pattern with a higher \nchance of showers/storms part of the day on Thursday. The other \nsolution pushes the longwave trough axis well east of the Atlantic\ncoast. This solution would mean drier and hotter weather on \nThursday with a low chance of showers/storms in the forecast. \n\nModel guidance does seem to converge on a solution that by Friday\nmorning the upper ridge to our south does strengthen and shift \nnortheast over the area. This should warm things up some more. \nDisturbances moving through NW flow well to our north should keep \nat least a low chance of showers and storms in the forecast over \nNE Alabama and southern middle Tennessee. Unfortunately, with this\nstrengthening of the upper ridge, 925 mb temperatures in most of\nthe guidance climb into a 25 to 28 degree range (GFS 28 to 29 \ndegree range). This would lend itself with good insolation to \nhighs climbing back into the 95 to 100 degree range unfortunately.\nWith surface winds originating from the Gulf of Mexico, dewpoints\nwill likely remain high. This may begin another mini-heat wave \nperiod over the area, where Heat products may be needed. \n\nModels diverge a bit on how much further north the upper ridge \nbuilds over the weekend and how much cloud cover/scattered \nconvection occurs. Which scenario holds true could have somewhat \nof a limiting effect on high temperatures and the heat. Either \nway, it will remain very hot and the heat wave could continue \nthrough the weekend.",
